htening: draft summary → critique for jargon/specificity → revise\n\n#### Alternative #2 — SequentialPipeline (confidence: 0.70)\nWhy this fits: A sequential pipeline fits the 'week-by-week' nature of the output if treated as a sequence of steps (e.g., generate schedule -> generate paces -> generate strength). However, iterative refinement is superior here because the parts are deeply interconnected (paces depend on altitude/age, strength depends on injury history).\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nSequentialPipeline is a :sequence of 2+ :llm/:code nodes where each step consumes the previous step's output. Natural for ETL (extract → enrich → format) and iterative refinement (draft → critique → revise). Avoid when stages are independent (use :parallel) or when 3+ small :llm steps could be one larger :llm — small successive :llm calls burn tokens that a single multi-step instruction would cover.\n\n\n#### Alternative #3 — SequentialPipeline (confidence: 0.70)\nWhy this fits: Provides the structural shape for ordered steps, which matches the multi-part output requirement, but lacks the specific 'refinement' logic that usually benefits complex training plans with medical/environmental constraints.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nSequentialPipeline is a :sequence of 2+ :llm/:code nodes where each step consumes the previous step's output. Natural for ETL (extract → enrich → format) and iterative refinement (draft → critique → revise). Avoid when stages are independent (use :parallel) or when 3+ small :llm steps could be one larger :llm — small successive :llm calls burn tokens that a single multi-step instruction would cover.\n\n\nAssigned to domain child b4ebfb53-2915-3390-a74a-adb9f1321de3 (label: marathon-training-plan) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 5 from corpus retrieval)\nClassifier reranker fell back on the behavioral axis; treat the suggestions below with caution.\n\n1. Behavioral: Research (confidence: 0.00) [reranker fell back to ColBERT — treat with caution]\n   Why this fits: \n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Research gathers raw external/contextual material to seed downstream behaviors. Best implemented as :parallel fan-out over 2-4 narrower queries (preserving source-id for downstream attribution) rather than one giant retrieve-everything query. ALWAYS provide a fallback path when retrieval is on the critical path — silent failure of external sources produces vacuous downstream output. Naturally composes into research-then-synthesize shells (sequential research → synthesis), briefing-generation (with structured per-source output), and fallback-recovery shells (primary source + backup). Avoid when all material is already in :reads or the task is purely structural.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - formulate one or more search/retrieval queries from the task goal, fan out queries, collect raw source material into a named :writes key\n  - natural fit at the start of a tree when the task needs evidence the model doesn't already have in context\n  - outputs are typically a vector of source snippets or a map of source-id → raw content for downstream extraction/synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** fan out independent queries with :parallel (or :map-each over queries) so wall-time stays bounded by the slowest single query, not the sum (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: queries are independent (no need to read result A before issuing query B) AND each query is cheap relative to the task budget\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:goal] :writes [:result-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:goal] :writes [:result-b]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** declare the source-type and shape in the :reads of downstream nodes so synthesis/analysis can branch on source